#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151802 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db871222-7398-5fea-8097-bcf669751a40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Polynomials that Differ by a Constant Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "78763507-6493-5a57-91a1-1c0c92324473" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14805" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $4(x+1)$ and $6(x+1)$." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(4,6)$:\n\n- Multiples of $4$: $\\quad 4, \\, 8, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\quad 6, \\, {\\color{blue}12}, \\, 18, \\ldots$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(4,6) = {\\color{blue}{12}}\n$$\n\nSince the variable part is the same in the expressions, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+1), \\, (x+1)\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x+1)}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(4(x+1), \\, 6(x+1)\\right) = {\\color{blue}{12}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x+1)}} = 12(x+1)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151804 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "92a06a13-9e6b-53b1-9fad-ec6cba8b9545" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Single-Variable Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "588a1460-6cd3-519a-ae98-7bc80c7a946c" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14809" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of $9(x-1)^4$ and $12(x-1)^2$." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of the variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(9,12)$:\n\n- Multiples of $9$: $\\quad 9, \\, 18, \\, 27, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 45, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $12$: $\\quad 12, \\, 24, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 48, \\ldots$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(9,12) = {\\color{blue}{36}}\n$$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x-1)^4$ and $(x-1)^2$, we raise $(x-1)$ to the largest power that appears in the expressions, which is $4$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x-1)^4, \\, (x-1)^2\\right) = {\\color{red}{(x-1)^4}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(9(x-1)^4, \\, 12(x-1)^2\\right) = {\\color{blue}{36}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{(x-1)^4}} = 36(x-1)^4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151805 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb9ff361-c15b-55e3-bd03-865ee6373d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Multivariable Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "9a1f928c-ab43-5d93-b187-13889113c46f" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14813" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $6(x+3)^2(y+1)^2$ and $9(x+3)(y+1)^4$." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(6,9)$:\n\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, {\\color{blue}18}, \\, 24, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $9$: $\\quad 9, \\, {\\color{blue}18}, \\, 27, \\ldots$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(6,9) = {\\color{blue}{18}}\n$$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+3)^2(y+1)^2$ and $(x+3)(y+1)^4$, we find the largest powers for $(x+3)$ and $(y+1)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(x+3)^2$ and $(x+3)$, the largest power is ${\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n- For $(y+1)^2$ and $(y+1)^4$, the largest power is ${\\color{red}{4}}$.\n\nNow, we raise $(x+3)$ and $(y+1)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+3)^2(y+1)^2, \\,(x+3)(y+1)^4\\right) =(x+3)^{\\color{blue}{2}}\\cdot (y+1)^{\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left(6(x+3)^2(y+1)^2, \\, 9(x+3)(y+1)^4\\right) = 18(x+3)^2 (y+1)^4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151806 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57496436-8bd0-567e-8c37-ed584849ce1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Three Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "3b462bac-92e9-5328-812c-da59d33b07f0" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-14815" :question/problem "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $12(x+2)^4 (y+1)$, $\\,9(x+2) (y+1)^2$, and $6(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3$." :question/worked-solution "To find the least common multiple ($\\textrm{LCM}$) of three polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nFirst, we find $\\textrm{LCM}(12, 9, 6)$:\n\n- Multiples of $12$: $\\quad 12, \\, 24, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 48, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $9$: $\\quad 9, \\, 18, \\, 27, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\, 45, \\ldots$\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\quad 6, \\, 12, \\, 18, \\, 24, \\, 30, \\, {\\color{blue}36}, \\ldots$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(12, 9, 6) = {\\color{blue}{36}}\n$$\n\nTo find the $\\textrm{LCM}$ of $(x+2)^4 (y+1), (x+2) (y+1)^2$, and $(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3$, we find the largest powers for $(x+2)$ and $(y+1)$ that appear in all these expressions:\n\n- For $(x+2)^4,(x+2)$, and $(x+2)^2$, the largest power is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$.\n- For $(y+1),(y+1)^2$, and $(y+1)^3$, the largest power is ${\\color{red}{3}}$.\n\nNow, we raise $(x+2)$ and $(y+1)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}\\left((x+2)^4 (y+1), \\,(x+2) (y+1)^2,\\,(x+2)^2 (y+1)^3\\right) =(x+2)^{\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot (y+1)^{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\nLCM(12(x + 2)^{4}(y + 1), 9(x + 2)(y + 1)^{2}, 6(x + 2)^{2}(y + 1)^{3}); = 36 \\cdot (x + 2)^{4} \\cdot (y + 1)^{3}; = 36(x + 2)^{4}(y + 1)^{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb9ff361-c15b-55e3-bd03-865ee6373d77" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LCM of Two Multivariable Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a5e8279-2aa1-5094-bab1-bd0305ffa7e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20u^3(v - 2)^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a5e8279-2aa1-5094-bab1-bd0305ffa7e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20u^3(v - 2)^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232195} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d4a028-71ca-5385-9c35-c1a5c0f6b36b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149501}] :question/id #uuid "46296e5e-dd83-5a74-9d94-7aa723a307ea" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-218516" :question/problem "The least common multiple of the polynomials $4u^{3}(v - 2)^{2}$ and $5u(v - 2)^{5}$ is {{field-1}}."}]}
